// Credentials — the ONE shape a package names its secret with: WHERE the credential lives, never its
// value. A workflow that must call an authenticated API declares a `credential` capability request; a
// PROVIDER discharges it by resolving a `credentials.source` (an `env` key or an `oauth` name) to the
// CURRENT value at each use. The app picks the source as DATA, so a rotation (`katari env set`) or an
// OAuth refresh lands without a restart, and the secret itself never enters the app's dataflow — it
// appears only inside the provider's handler, as a `string of private` bound for a submission sink.
//
// No real secret is needed to READ this: it typechecks the composition — the shapes are the lesson,
// like `scoped.ktr`. `main` exercises only the DATA side (naming sources), which needs no live
// credential; `provider` and `authorized_get` typecheck to lock the resolve / header-building shapes.

@"The capability an imaginary API client requires; a provider discharges it. Its `string of private`
result may flow ONLY toward a submission sink (an http `Authorization` header), never to a run result."
request credential() -> string of private

@"Discharge `credential` by resolving @source@ at EACH performance — the standard package provider
shape (tavily / imagegen / google_calendar all share it). Per-call resolution is why an OAuth refresh
or an env rotation is picked up with no restart; the app decides `env` vs `oauth`, and this provider
never learns which."
agent provider[R, effect E](
  source: credentials.source,
  continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, credential},
) -> R with E | io | prelude.throw[env.missing_secret | oauth.server_error] {
  use handler {
    request credential() -> string of private { next credentials.resolve(source = source) }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"A call the client makes: resolve `credential` and put it in the `Authorization` header — the ONE
place a secret may flow (concatenating a private value keeps the result private). Structural: `http.fetch`
needs a live endpoint and token, so `main` does not call this; it typechecks to lock the header shape."
agent authorized_get(url: string) -> string with credential | io | prelude.throw[http.fetch_error] {
  let bearer : string of private = credential()
  let headers = record.set(
    target = record.empty(),
    key = "Authorization",
    value = prelude.concat(left = "Bearer ", right = bearer),
  )
  let response = http.fetch(url = url, method = "GET", headers = headers, body = http.text(content = ""))
  response.body
}

@"Entry: the APP side — name where each integration's credential lives, as data. No secret is touched
(these are names), so this is deterministic. Installing `provider(source = ...)` around a body that
performs `credential` is all it takes to authenticate it — `authorized_get` above is such a body."
agent main() -> string {
  // The app's choice, as data: a simple API key from the env store, or a runtime-managed OAuth
  // credential by name. The package consuming `credential` never learns which it got.
  let from_env : credentials.source = credentials.env(key = "EXAMPLE_API_KEY")
  let from_oauth : credentials.source = credentials.oauth(name = "example-service")
  let described = for (let source in [from_env, from_oauth]) {
    next match (source) {
      case credentials.env(key => key) -> f"env:${key}"
      case credentials.oauth(name => name) -> f"oauth:${name}"
    }
  }
  string.join(parts = described, separator = " | ")
}
